          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Curriculum Scope: Isolated DC-DC Topologies: Flyback, Forward, and Push-Pull Converters",
          "content": "# Unit 1: Single-phase inverters and waveform synthesis\n\n## Engineering situation\n\nA solar-powered isolated test bench requires a controllable AC waveform from a DC source. The switching bridge does not create energy: it rearranges source polarity at the terminals, with output filtering and load dynamics determining the current. Treat the requested 50 Hz fundamental separately from switching-frequency components and never confuse an RMS specification with a peak value.\n\n## Shared numerical scenario\n\nIn this unit, the shared design case is a 48 V DC source and a resistive 16 \u03a9 educational load. An ideal full bridge can apply +48 V, 0 V or \u221248 V depending on the gating scheme. The bridge is modeled as ideal to isolate modulation concepts; losses, dead time and bus ripple are added as limitations, not hidden inside an unexplained efficiency coefficient.\n\n## Intended competence\n\nA learner should be able to reconstruct a switching function, compare two PWM methods, derive an approximate fundamental amplitude, and explain why a filter cannot be designed from only an average voltage. The bridge must never be connected to the utility supply or a household outlet.\n\n## Assumptions and model boundary\n\nThis unit uses specific equations at specific operating points; a model must not be extended merely because it produces a number outside the stated range. In particular, the switching network may be averaged over a PWM period for a slow state calculation, while magnetic reset, terminal-voltage stress and semiconductor switching energy demand individual switching intervals. Keep the mechanical, magnetic and electrical energy variables separate where appropriate. Every diagram should identify a reference node, current direction and whether the axes show peak, average, or RMS quantities. Where an external network or motor is discussed, the waveform is software-generated for learning and does not authorize connection to an energized system.\n\n## Unit reading route\n\nRead Lessons 1.1 through 1.4 in sequence, then use the [unit simulation](../simulations/model-01.html). Complete the unit project before the assessment. Preserve the original scenario as a baseline so parameter changes are attributable; do not change source, loading and control law simultaneously. When citing a numerical result, write the values, units and limit cases, then label it as ideal analytical, simplified dynamic model, computed simulation or actual measurement. No measurement claims are made here.\n\n## Technical review checkpoint\n\nSeparate a switching-period average, a fundamental-frequency RMS value, and the instantaneous device stress. The first predicts slow energy transfer, the second predicts sinusoidal heating of the useful component, and the third can determine semiconductor survivability. Requiring these three outputs from one simplistic formula is a modeling error.\n\nRecord one PWM-period zoom, at least two complete fundamental cycles, and a separate worst-case turn-off event in a software waveform study. Use the same voltage reference point in every comparison. Report the spectral window and whether the original waveform is periodic over that interval.\n\nA good explanation specifies the assumptions under which a relation is true and names an experiment or simulation capable of detecting when it fails. A numerical waveform can agree with its own programmed equations and still be incapable of predicting a real apparatus. Use the following assessment to distinguish those two forms of evidence.\n"
