          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Curriculum Scope: Resonant Converters: LLC Resonant Tanks, Zero-Voltage and Zero-Current Switching",
          "content": "# Unit 3: Motor-drive converters and regenerative operation\n\n## Engineering situation\n\nA motor drive coordinates electromagnetic torque, mechanical inertia, power-stage limits and a DC-link energy balance. Instantaneous electrical power can reverse during braking, but the DC source must have an actual path for the returned energy. A commanded negative torque is not evidence that a non-regenerative supply can absorb power.\n\n## Shared numerical scenario\n\nWe compare a simple scalar volts-per-hertz model with a field-oriented control block diagram. The examples assume idealized motor parameters for teaching; a real commissioning exercise requires motor identification, current sensors, thermal ratings, fault management and guarded rotating equipment.\n\n## Intended competence\n\nLearners will calculate synchronous speed and slip, separate torque-producing and flux-producing current concepts, audit regenerative energy and determine why bus overvoltage requires a protective strategy independent of the speed controller.\n\n## Assumptions and model boundary\n\nThis unit uses specific equations at specific operating points; a model must not be extended merely because it produces a number outside the stated range. In particular, the switching network may be averaged over a PWM period for a slow state calculation, while magnetic reset, terminal-voltage stress and semiconductor switching energy demand individual switching intervals. Keep the mechanical, magnetic and electrical energy variables separate where appropriate. Every diagram should identify a reference node, current direction and whether the axes show peak, average, or RMS quantities. Where an external network or motor is discussed, the waveform is software-generated for learning and does not authorize connection to an energized system.\n\n## Unit reading route\n\nRead Lessons 3.1 through 3.4 in sequence, then use the [unit simulation](../simulations/model-03.html). Complete the unit project before the assessment. Preserve the original scenario as a baseline so parameter changes are attributable; do not change source, loading and control law simultaneously. When citing a numerical result, write the values, units and limit cases, then label it as ideal analytical, simplified dynamic model, computed simulation or actual measurement. No measurement claims are made here.\n\n## Technical review checkpoint\n\nThe useful control output in a drive is mechanical torque or speed, but the actuator is an electrical converter. Energy travels through magnetic fields and rotor inertia. During braking the direction reverses, exposing any assumption that a DC source is a two-way energy sink.\n\nUse separate vertical axes for q-axis current, speed, electromagnetic torque and DC-link voltage. State the assumptions that link them, including machine orientation, inertia, torque constant and converter loss. A single attractive speed trace does not validate the energy balance.\n\nA good explanation specifies the assumptions under which a relation is true and names an experiment or simulation capable of detecting when it fails. A numerical waveform can agree with its own programmed equations and still be incapable of predicting a real apparatus. Use the following assessment to distinguish those two forms of evidence.\n"
