          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Curriculum Scope: Single-Phase Voltage-Source Inverters and Sinusoidal Pulse-Width Modulation",
          "content": "# Unit 4: Isolated converters and transformer constraints\n\n## Engineering situation\n\nGalvanic isolation changes the available circuit architecture but does not make an energized output automatically safe. A flyback device stores energy in a coupled magnetic element; a forward or bridge converter transfers energy during a different interval. Transformer turns ratios alone cannot determine the operating point without the switch timing and reset conditions.\n\n## Shared numerical scenario\n\nOur case is a conceptual 24 V source powering a low-voltage isolated sensor supply. We examine energy per cycle, peak flux density, winding copper loss, insulation assumptions and reset constraints. Hardware construction details and safety isolation certification are outside the scope of this book.\n\n## Intended competence\n\nLearners will explain magnetizing current, distinguish energy-storage and transformer-action topologies, identify output rectifier timing and produce a safety review that lists required but unavailable material properties and manufacturing evidence.\n\n## Assumptions and model boundary\n\nThis unit uses specific equations at specific operating points; a model must not be extended merely because it produces a number outside the stated range. In particular, the switching network may be averaged over a PWM period for a slow state calculation, while magnetic reset, terminal-voltage stress and semiconductor switching energy demand individual switching intervals. Keep the mechanical, magnetic and electrical energy variables separate where appropriate. Every diagram should identify a reference node, current direction and whether the axes show peak, average, or RMS quantities. Where an external network or motor is discussed, the waveform is software-generated for learning and does not authorize connection to an energized system.\n\n## Unit reading route\n\nRead Lessons 4.1 through 4.4 in sequence, then use the [unit simulation](../simulations/model-04.html). Complete the unit project before the assessment. Preserve the original scenario as a baseline so parameter changes are attributable; do not change source, loading and control law simultaneously. When citing a numerical result, write the values, units and limit cases, then label it as ideal analytical, simplified dynamic model, computed simulation or actual measurement. No measurement claims are made here.\n\n## Technical review checkpoint\n\nA coupled magnetic device has at least three different duties: transfer or store magnetic energy, prevent flux walk, and maintain physical separation between conductors. A calculated volts-per-turn value may address the first two but cannot establish that winding insulation withstands the required service environment.\n\nFor every switch interval mark applied primary voltage, magnetizing-current direction, secondary-diode condition and stored energy change. Then make an additional, intentionally incomplete insulation checklist. Do not fill missing material or test data with an assumed pass result.\n\nA good explanation specifies the assumptions under which a relation is true and names an experiment or simulation capable of detecting when it fails. A numerical waveform can agree with its own programmed equations and still be incapable of predicting a real apparatus. Use the following assessment to distinguish those two forms of evidence.\n"
