          "content": "# Unit 5: Resonant and soft-switching conversion\n\n## Engineering situation\n\nResonant networks trade frequency-dependent impedance against switching transitions and circulating current. In a resonant inverter, the impedance near resonance can amplify current even when useful load power is modest. Zero-voltage switching and zero-current switching refer to transitions at particular instants; neither proves zero overall loss.\n\n## Shared numerical scenario\n\nA normalized series RLC example anchors this unit. Starting from L=10 \u00b5H and C=100 nF, we calculate a resonance frequency and explore detuning, quality factor and sensitivity. The simulator stays linear and sinusoidal; it does not pretend to solve every nonlinear LLC operating mode.\n\n## Intended competence\n\nLearners will locate resonance, distinguish series resonance from LLC magnetizing behavior, reason about soft-switching boundaries and compare a loss reduction with the added magnetics and control complexity.\n\n## Assumptions and model boundary\n\nThis unit uses specific equations at specific operating points; a model must not be extended merely because it produces a number outside the stated range. In particular, the switching network may be averaged over a PWM period for a slow state calculation, while magnetic reset, terminal-voltage stress and semiconductor switching energy demand individual switching intervals. Keep the mechanical, magnetic and electrical energy variables separate where appropriate. Every diagram should identify a reference node, current direction and whether the axes show peak, average, or RMS quantities. Where an external network or motor is discussed, the waveform is software-generated for learning and does not authorize connection to an energized system.\n\n## Unit reading route\n\nRead Lessons 5.1 through 5.4 in sequence, then use the [unit simulation](../simulations/model-05.html). Complete the unit project before the assessment. Preserve the original scenario as a baseline so parameter changes are attributable; do not change source, loading and control law simultaneously. When citing a numerical result, write the values, units and limit cases, then label it as ideal analytical, simplified dynamic model, computed simulation or actual measurement. No measurement claims are made here.\n\n## Technical review checkpoint\n\nResonant gain is a function of frequency and loading rather than a single fixed transformer turns ratio. A soft-switching assumption can be true at one operating point and false at another. The most dangerous regions may be startup and light load, not maximum delivered power.\n\nPlot voltage gain or current magnitude on a frequency axis using fixed, documented component values; repeat with at least two load values. Locate the resonant peak, mark the chosen command range, and explain what a linear model cannot tell you about switch-node commutation.\n\nA good explanation specifies the assumptions under which a relation is true and names an experiment or simulation capable of detecting when it fails. A numerical waveform can agree with its own programmed equations and still be incapable of predicting a real apparatus. Use the following assessment to distinguish those two forms of evidence.\n"
