          "heading": "Engineering Design Project & Numerical Investigation: Multi-Level Inverter Topologies: Diode-Clamped, Flying-Capacitor, and Cascaded H-Bridge",
          "content": "# Unit 6 project: PFC input waveform and energy-buffer review\n\n**Scope:** software-only engineering study and analytical notebook. No mains, traction battery, energized utility interface, or uncontrolled rotating machine is authorized. **Deliverables:** calculation sheet, documented assumptions, parameter sweep, self-check plot, uncertainty register and short technical decision record.\n\n## Project brief\n\nFor a 230 V RMS 50 Hz mathematical source, analyze a power-factor case with I_rms=10 A, fundamental current I1=8 A and displacement factor 0.95. Compute real power, apparent power and true PF. Then analyze the twice-line-frequency energy variation for a separate ideal unity-PF 1000 W case.\n\nBegin with a clear control volume and state where power enters and exits. Draw the useful storage variables alongside the PWM switching states. Specify both time scales that matter: a switching or sampled control step, and the slower physical response. Fix a baseline and use identical physical units in every table. Give each graph explicit axis units and distinguish the dependent variable from the manipulated independent parameter.\n\n## Experiment 1 \u2014 reproduce the baseline\n\nCalculate all baseline quantities by hand or a reproducible script using the equations in Lessons 6.1\u20136.4. Retain three significant figures before rounding. Use a dimensional cross-check and, where possible, an energy-conservation check. If the model has only a steady-state answer, state why it cannot predict transient overshoot or a semiconductor edge event. Record a short comment explaining whether the computed output is peak, RMS, mean or instantaneous.\n\n## Experiment 2 \u2014 a controlled perturbation\n\nCompare 500, 1000 and 2000 \u00b5F link capacitors under the linear small-ripple 400 V DC approximation. Plot predicted link peak-to-peak ripple and calculate stored energy at the nominal DC voltage. Identify what this suggests about startup and hold-up requirements.\n\nAn informative parameter sweep changes one physical variable at a time and makes the boundary of the model visible. Where the response is proportional to a square, plot both the direct input and its square so a linear relationship in the transformed variable is evident. Where frequency is varied, avoid mixing hertz and radians per second. Add a small error budget that separates rounding, parameter tolerance and explicitly unmodeled physics. Never present the neatness of a plotted line as independent validation of a physical circuit.\n\n## Deliverable and evidence standard\n\nProvide explicit definitions of RMS total current versus fundamental current, a calculation of p(t), a capacitor-energy argument and a fault-scenario discussion. No student lab is permitted to connect to 230 V mains.\n\nInclude a table with columns: claim; governing equation; assumed parameter; predicted number; sensitivity to a changed parameter; required physical measurement; status. At least three rows must be marked 'analytical estimate', one must be marked 'simulation only', and two must be marked 'unverified without real data'. If a control function is said to protect a system, describe the *software state* that changes; do not imply that a high-energy circuit is safely disconnected by an untested algorithm.\n\n## Critical decision memo\n\nWrite 300\u2013500 words comparing the baseline design against one alternative, showing what improves, what becomes more difficult and what would stop a release. Quantify at least one tradeoff. A technically credible answer may decline to rank options when necessary thermal or component properties were not specified. In that case identify the least-cost experiment or authoritative datasheet information that would resolve the ambiguity. Conclude with an operating-region sketch rather than an unsupported single optimum.\n\n## Instructor solution and boundary conditions\n\nFor the first case, P=1748 W, S=2300 VA, true PF\u22480.76. The separate 1000 W unity-PF case has a low-frequency energy amplitude about 1.59 J; 1000 \u00b5F near 400 V gives around 3.98 V ripple amplitude under linearization.\n\nAn acceptable submission reproduces the numerical baseline independently; makes the units and sign conventions unambiguous; describes what extra physical or sampled-time behavior could invalidate the results; and includes an explicit exclusion of hardware safety certification. An incorrect submission treats a nominal fundamental voltage as total broadband RMS, mistakes a mathematical natural frequency for an empirical stability limit, or declares a component safe without its applicable manufacturer ratings. Project success demonstrates reasoning and traceable calculation, not a built converter.\n"
