          "heading": "Foundational Principles & Curriculum Scope: Grid-Connected Renewable Inverters, LCL Filters, and Synchronization Control",
          "content": "# Unit 8: Wide-bandgap devices, reliability and engineering verification\n\n## Engineering situation\n\nWide-bandgap devices allow different blocking voltages and switching speeds from conventional silicon devices, but fast transitions can expose parasitic inductance, gate-loop susceptibility, insulation stress and measurement artifacts. A smaller nominal switching-loss number is not by itself evidence of a reliable product.\n\n## Shared numerical scenario\n\nThe culminating case is a simulated high-efficiency converter design review. A team must compare two abstract switches, a thermal impedance estimate, cycling fatigue assumptions and an EMI verification plan without inventing a manufacturer\u2019s qualified ratings or a standards certificate.\n\n## Intended competence\n\nLearners will build a traceable loss table, calculate basic thermal headroom under declared assumptions, distinguish lifetime testing from an Arrhenius-style screening estimate, and explain the difference between a passing model and a validated physical converter.\n\n## Assumptions and model boundary\n\nThis unit uses specific equations at specific operating points; a model must not be extended merely because it produces a number outside the stated range. In particular, the switching network may be averaged over a PWM period for a slow state calculation, while magnetic reset, terminal-voltage stress and semiconductor switching energy demand individual switching intervals. Keep the mechanical, magnetic and electrical energy variables separate where appropriate. Every diagram should identify a reference node, current direction and whether the axes show peak, average, or RMS quantities. Where an external network or motor is discussed, the waveform is software-generated for learning and does not authorize connection to an energized system.\n\n## Unit reading route\n\nRead Lessons 8.1 through 8.4 in sequence, then use the [unit simulation](../simulations/model-08.html). Complete the unit project before the assessment. Preserve the original scenario as a baseline so parameter changes are attributable; do not change source, loading and control law simultaneously. When citing a numerical result, write the values, units and limit cases, then label it as ideal analytical, simplified dynamic model, computed simulation or actual measurement. No measurement claims are made here.\n\n## Technical review checkpoint\n\nComponent comparison should hold equal the electrical operating points and reporting conditions. A device with attractive room-temperature resistance may perform poorly when hot; lower switching energy can be offset by unwanted parasitic current or additional filtering requirements. Reliability claims require a mission profile, not one efficiency measurement.\n\nBuild a claims table with columns for assumption, derivation, simulated check, measurement still required and responsible reviewer. Treat every blank measurement as unresolved, never as an implied pass.\n\nA good explanation specifies the assumptions under which a relation is true and names an experiment or simulation capable of detecting when it fails. A numerical waveform can agree with its own programmed equations and still be incapable of predicting a real apparatus. Use the following assessment to distinguish those two forms of evidence.\n"
